[Unit]
Description=Synthetic Enterprise seat-executor timer — activates seat-executor.service, offering the seat's handed-off work to an unattended turn every 30 minutes. The unit it starts is named here rather than left to systemd's name-derivation: this file IS the schedule, and a reader (or a control asserting that exactly one declared thing invokes the executor) should not have to know the convention to see what it fires.

[Timer]
# THIRTY MINUTES, matching the worker tick, and the cadence is bounded by material change rather
# than by this clock: with nothing handed off and no unclaimed focus item the service stands down
# on a cheap read and spawns no model turn at all. The expensive case only arises when the
# interactive seat has actually written a continuation, which is a deliberate act.
#
# :05 and :35, deliberately OFF the tick's :00/:30 and off delivery-seat's :20. Three writers
# arriving in the same second is how a claim store gets read half-written, and the three-hour
# orientation is the one that must not be read mid-stretch (see delivery-seat.timer).
OnCalendar=*-*-* *:05,35:00
Persistent=false
RandomizedDelaySec=120

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target
